    "en":  {
               "headline":  "CRDO drops 15% to $99.86 in AI-chip sell-off ahead of Dec 1 earnings",
               "technical":  "The trend is clearly down. At $99.86, CRDO is far below its EMA50 ($161.04), EMA100 ($177.60) and EMA200 ($175.86). These are exponential moving averages, which weight recent days more heavily. The EMA50 has fallen below both longer averages, and the EMA100 has edged under the EMA200 line, so the longer-term trend is also turning lower. MACD, a trend-momentum gauge, is −16.22, below its signal line at −15.00. The histogram is −1.22, which means selling momentum picked up again instead of fading. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge where under 30 means oversold) is 31.61, so the stock is close to oversold but not there yet. Price is pressing the lower Bollinger Band at $98.91 (bands sit 2 standard deviations around the 20-day average), and the intraday low of $97.92 dipped below it. The band runs from $98.91 to $174.04, a very wide range that shows extreme volatility. Volume was 13.1M shares, about 1.6× the 20-day average, so heavy trading backed the selling. The stock opened at $114.01 and closed near the day\u0027s low. Support is $97.92–98.91, then the 52-week low of $86.49. Resistance is $114–117.76, then the band midline at $136.47. The stock is 68% below its 52-week high of $308.67.",
               "fundamental":  "The business is still growing fast. Fiscal Q1 2027 (quarter ended August 1, 2026) revenue was $479M, up 115% year on year, with record optical DSP and retimer sales. Guidance for fiscal Q2 is $525–535M at a 67–69% non-GAAP gross margin. There are also warning signs. Operating margin fell to 25.2% from 35.7% the quarter before, and inventory rose $62.2M to $313.1M. The top four customers made up 84% of revenue, and the largest alone was 33%. In the AI value chain, Credo sells the short copper and optical links that connect GPUs, switches and racks. Its edge is SerDes chip design, and it holds an estimated 93–99% share of active electrical cables (AECs). At the November 20 price, the stock trades at about 15× forward earnings, against a 3-year average of 61×. The market is pricing in lower share or growth, not a broken business.",
               "news":  [
                            "Nov 20, 2026: CRDO fell 15.2% to $99.86 in a broad tech sell-off. XLK, the tech-sector ETF, fell about 6%. The trigger was California\u0027s attorney general subpoenaing Nvidia after the Nov 19 close over whether its chips or confidential information reached China. No Credo-specific news came out that day.",
                            "Nov 20, 2026: Stifel cut its price target to $250 from $310 but kept a Buy rating, and said it still expects Credo to beat and raise guidance on Dec 1.",
                            "Nov 17, 2026: Credo and Marvell settled their AEC patent dispute, which also ended Marvell\u0027s challenges to Credo patents at the USPTO. Terms are confidential. Shares rose about 6% premarket on Nov 18.",
                            "Nov 11, 2026: Credo said it will report fiscal Q2 2027 results (quarter ended Oct 31) after the close on Dec 1, 2026, with a call at 2:00 p.m. PT.",
                            "Oct–Nov 2026: Competition fears weighed on the stock. Reports said Cisco is seeking quotes for its own proprietary 1.6T cable program. MaxLinear\u0027s CEO said the company is targeting 20–30% of the 1.6T AEC market, and Edgewater Research saw little sign of near-term 1.6T AEC deployments.",
                            "Oct 12, 2026: At the OCP Global Summit, Credo launched the 1.6T ZeroFlap Active Light Cable. It is an optical cable for runs up to 50 m that draws 12 W per end, and shipping is planned for 2027."
                        ],
               "caution":  "Both trend and momentum still point down, and the MACD histogram turned more negative on November 20 on heavy volume. If the $97.92 low gives way, the next clear reference is the 52-week low of $86.49. Results on December 1 are a two-way risk for a stock with a five-year beta of about 3.3. Investors will check whether the 84% customer concentration and rising inventory signal slower orders, and whether Cisco or MaxLinear are winning 1.6T designs. Comments on 1.6T timing will matter as much as the headline numbers.",
               "opportunity":  "The November 20 drop was driven by Nvidia news and a sector-wide sell-off, not by anything Credo reported. Meanwhile, the Marvell settlement on November 17 removed a legal overhang from the AEC franchise. RSI at 31.61 and price at the lower Bollinger Band show the stock is stretched to the downside. At about 15× forward earnings, expectations are low compared with revenue growth above 100%. If Q2 lands at or above the $525–535M guide and the optical push, including the Active Light Cable, gains traction, the valuation gap could narrow.",
               "risks":  [
                             "Customer concentration: the top four customers were 84% of fiscal Q1 revenue, with no long-term purchase commitments",
                             "Competition in 1.6T AECs from MaxLinear (targeting 20–30% share) and a possible proprietary Cisco cable program",
                             "Margin and inventory pressure: operating margin fell to 25.2%, and inventory rose to $313.1M",
                             "High beta (about 3.3) makes the stock very sensitive to Nvidia news, AI-capex worries and data-center policy",
                             "Earnings event risk on Dec 1, 2026, at a time when the technical trend is weak"
                         ],
               "summary":  "The technical posture is bearish. CRDO trades well below all three EMAs, MACD is negative and widening, and the November 20 drop of 15.2% came on about 1.6× normal volume. Price now sits at the lower Bollinger Band, with RSI near oversold. The latest leg down came from Nvidia-related sector selling, not from company news, and the Marvell patent settlement is a real positive. However, competition in 1.6T cables and heavy customer concentration are open questions. Until the December 1 results answer them, the moat thesis is on watch, not broken."
